            <fr:title text="Height at n ≤ 7">Height at n ≤ 7</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">height</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">journal thm:height-n7 (fourteenth pass); census 275/276/277 plus the diamond; verified against Goddard--Goodwin and GMR2.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">The height conjecture</fr:link> holds for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  7]]></fr:tex> and
every prime power <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>, unconditionally.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> The modality theorem: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n}) = 1]]></fr:tex> for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 6, 7]]></fr:tex>. With
at most one modulus of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-orbits on the nilradical, the
two-moduli case of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link> never arises, so
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">the orbital-variety theorem</fr:link> closes every subvariety. The
modality inputs are published rows discharged against machine
certificates (Goddard--Goodwin; GMR2).</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">The height conjecture</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">Orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0027/" title="Borel modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0027/" display-uri="ffi-0027" type="local">Borel modality</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0013</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension">The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>principle</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 2 (the avatar structure).</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Every bound the program needs is half of a nilpotent orbit dimension.
Nilpotent orbits carry the Kostant--Kirillov form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> in every
characteristic; a subvariety of half dimension inside an orbit sits at
the Lagrangian bound, and dimension <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\le ]]></fr:tex> half is exactly what isotropy
forces.</html:p>
            <html:p>The thesis appears at three scales, and the three avatars of the
forest are its three instances.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><html:strong>Global</html:strong> — the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> form on each orbit:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Semi-local</html:strong> — the residue form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex> on
  adjoint torsion at drop points: <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Infinitesimal</html:strong> — the survivor cocycle at a pencil germ:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>, a conjecture.</html:li></html:ul>
            <html:p>This is a principle, not a theorem: it is the reason the three
avatars have the same shape, and it is what tells you that
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link> is the right thing to be stuck on.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0012/" title="The three recurring numbers" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/" display-uri="ffi-0012" type="local">The three recurring numbers</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0011</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0011/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The trace form">The trace form</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex sec:kk-lagrangian.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The <html:em>trace form</html:em> on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {gl}_n]]></fr:tex>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \kappa (A, B) = \operatorname {tr}(AB),
]]></fr:tex>
perfect over every ring, symmetric, and invariant:
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa ([P, A], B) = -\kappa (A, [P, B])]]></fr:tex>. Twisted, it
identifies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {gl}_n]]></fr:tex> with its <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex>-twisted dual.</html:p>
            <html:p>Restricted to the commutator subspace of a fixed <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex>, its
alternation <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\kappa ([P, A], A) = 0]]></fr:tex> is the source of every
symplectic structure in the program. The Kostant--Kirillov form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex>
that makes <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">orbital varieties Lagrangian</fr:link>, the residue form
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex> of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link>, and the survivor cocycle of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0017/" title="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/" display-uri="ffi-0017" type="local">Wall 1</fr:link> are all restrictions of it.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0055/" title="The entrywise Frobenius" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0055/" display-uri="ffi-0055" type="local">The entrywise Frobenius</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/sec-symplectic/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>sec-symplectic</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/sec-symplectic/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest › The symplectic skeleton"><fr:link href="/ffi/index/" title="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/index/" display-uri="index" type="local">finite-field-invariants: a reference forest</fr:link> › The symplectic skeleton</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>One idea at three scales. Avatar 1 is global, avatar 2 semi-local,
  avatar 3 infinitesimal; the thesis is that each is the same statement
  about half an orbit dimension.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link> — the pairing everything is measured
    in.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0012/" title="The three recurring numbers" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/" display-uri="ffi-0012" type="local">The three numbers</fr:link> — centralizer, orbit, and the
    half.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">The thesis</fr:link> — the thesis, and the three avatars.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">Orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link> — avatar 1,
    proven.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0015/" title="Nilpotent pencils, drop points, the centralizer sheaf" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0015/" display-uri="ffi-0015" type="local">The pencil apparatus</fr:link> — the semi-local apparatus.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link> — avatar 2, proven.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0049/" title="The germ apparatus" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0049/" display-uri="ffi-0049" type="local">The germ apparatus</fr:link> — avatar 3, the germ apparatus.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0017/" title="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/" display-uri="ffi-0017" type="local">Wall 1</fr:link> — the bottom floor of the tower.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0051/" title="The anomaly law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0051/" display-uri="ffi-0051" type="local">The anomaly law</fr:link> — why characteristic 2.</html:li></html:ul>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
