is an orbital variety; what is left is the case of subvarieties carrying
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\ge  2]]></fr:tex> moduli of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-orbits, where characteristic <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p]]></fr:tex> denies
the generic-smoothness argument. First possible at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 6]]></fr:tex>, which is
why the ladder <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0018/" title="Height at n ≤ 9" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0018/" display-uri="ffi-0018" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex></fr:link> has to be climbed by
hand.</html:p>
            <html:p>The infinitesimal form of the same statement is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">Orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0009</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0009/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The height conjecture">The height conjecture</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="area">height</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 1; journal thm:height-n7, thm:m8, cor:height-n8, cor:height-n9.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> and every prime power <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {ht} J = \frac {n(n+1)}{2},
  \qquad  \text {equivalently} \qquad 
  \dim  V(J) = \frac {n(n - 1)}{2}:
]]></fr:tex>
the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {n(n+1)}{2}]]></fr:tex> invariants <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}(c_{j})]]></fr:tex>,
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0 \le  i < j \le  n]]></fr:tex>, form a regular sequence in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> and extend to a
homogeneous system of parameters. The conjectured dimension is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  \mathrm {Fl}]]></fr:tex> — up to dimension, the
flag witnesses must be everything.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Status.</html:strong> Proven for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  5]]></fr:tex> every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>
(<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0019/" title="Height at n ≤ 5" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0019/" display-uri="ffi-0019" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  5]]></fr:tex></fr:link>); for characteristic <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex>, every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>;
for all but finitely many characteristics per <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>, ineffectively; and
for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex> every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex> (<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0018/" title="Height at n ≤ 9" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0018/" display-uri="ffi-0018" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex></fr:link>). Open in
general, and what is open is exactly <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">The Steenrod ideal</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0008/" title="The Krull bound: only the upper bound is open" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0008/" display-uri="ffi-0008" type="local">The Krull bound: only the upper bound is open</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0013</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension">The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>principle</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 2 (the avatar structure).</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Every bound the program needs is half of a nilpotent orbit dimension.
Nilpotent orbits carry the Kostant--Kirillov form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> in every
characteristic; a subvariety of half dimension inside an orbit sits at
the Lagrangian bound, and dimension <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\le ]]></fr:tex> half is exactly what isotropy
forces.</html:p>
            <html:p>The thesis appears at three scales, and the three avatars of the
forest are its three instances.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><html:strong>Global</html:strong> — the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> form on each orbit:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Semi-local</html:strong> — the residue form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex> on
  adjoint torsion at drop points: <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Infinitesimal</html:strong> — the survivor cocycle at a pencil germ:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>, a conjecture.</html:li></html:ul>
            <html:p>This is a principle, not a theorem: it is the reason the three
avatars have the same shape, and it is what tells you that
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link> is the right thing to be stuck on.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0012/" title="The three recurring numbers" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/" display-uri="ffi-0012" type="local">The three recurring numbers</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0012</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0012/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The three recurring numbers">The three recurring numbers</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>lemma</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">standard; journal sec:orbits.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Fix <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda  \vdash  n]]></fr:tex> with conjugate <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda ']]></fr:tex>. Then
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  c(\lambda ) = \sum _{i,j} \min (\lambda _i, \lambda _j)
             = \sum _j (\lambda '_j)^2, \qquad 
  \dim  \mathcal {O}_{\lambda } = n^2 - c(\lambda ), \qquad 
  \dim  \mathrm {Fl}_{\lambda '} = \frac {1}{2} \dim  \mathcal {O}_{\lambda }.
]]></fr:tex></html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c(\lambda )]]></fr:tex> is the dimension of the centralizer of a
nilpotent of type <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda ]]></fr:tex>, in every characteristic: the centralizer
is the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k[T]]]></fr:tex>-module <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {Hom}]]></fr:tex> of the type modules, whose dimension is
the stated minimum-sum. For regular <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda ]]></fr:tex> the half is
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {n(n - 1)}{2}]]></fr:tex> — the height-conjecture dimension, which is why
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">the height conjecture</fr:link> and <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link> are the same
statement at two scales.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0011</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0011/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The trace form">The trace form</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex sec:kk-lagrangian.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The <html:em>trace form</html:em> on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {gl}_n]]></fr:tex>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \kappa (A, B) = \operatorname {tr}(AB),
]]></fr:tex>
perfect over every ring, symmetric, and invariant:
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa ([P, A], B) = -\kappa (A, [P, B])]]></fr:tex>. Twisted, it
identifies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {gl}_n]]></fr:tex> with its <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex>-twisted dual.</html:p>
            <html:p>Restricted to the commutator subspace of a fixed <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex>, its
alternation <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\kappa ([P, A], A) = 0]]></fr:tex> is the source of every
symplectic structure in the program. The Kostant--Kirillov form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex>
that makes <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">orbital varieties Lagrangian</fr:link>, the residue form
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex> of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link>, and the survivor cocycle of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0017/" title="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/" display-uri="ffi-0017" type="local">Wall 1</fr:link> are all restrictions of it.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0055/" title="The entrywise Frobenius" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0055/" display-uri="ffi-0055" type="local">The entrywise Frobenius</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
